A||N||5||general||V-6 idle setting||||5||
Z||000000||general||04-17-2000||10:19 PM||||I have an '86SE that I have just recently rebuilt.I finally got the car to crank and am in the process of trying to fine tune the timing.When I get it to crank though,it will rev up to about 2500-3000 rpm's then it dies.It will not idle by itself at all.I checked out the diagnostic codes and the only one that shows is the MAP sensor curcuit.I have checked out the vacuum lines and they seem O.K.. I am also preparing to replace the MAP sensor.My question is this;Will replacing this sensor help the idle at all? Or do I need to be looking elsewhere? Any help at all will be appreciated.     Thanks!   general||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||04-17-2000||10:57 PM||||The MAP sensor could be bad or your IAC valve could be bad. If you have a vacuum leak somewhere like the EGR tube, EGR or the intake itself, it will mess up the idle and trip a MAP sensor code. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Linx||04-18-2000||10:36 AM||||I killed myself over the same problem.  I too, beat myself up over a vacuum leak. Looked over everything...and then found that 5/8" hose that goes between the lower manifold and the throttle plate.  I'd looked at it before and it was snug enough, or so it seemed.  Replaceing it (and the replacement was waaaaay tight) did the trick.||||reg||3
Z||000003||theogre||04-18-2000||12:41 PM||||Vacuum leaks anywhere in the system can cause MAP related codes to set. You didn't say which code you got.<P>You can test MAP if you have an <B>accurate</B> vac gauge, an ECM scanner or DVM, and a hand vacuum pump.<P>I forget the exact number it should read. The test vacuum is 10in/hg. I'll have to look the output number up later.||||reg||1
Z||000004||general||04-20-2000||03:35 PM||||Thanks for the help guys. I replaced the MAP sensor and the idle problem went away.The diagnostic code still shows a 33( MAP sensor code)which may be slight vacuum leak.  general||||reg||7
Z||000005||Patrick||04-20-2000||09:16 PM||||<BR>Did you clear the code 33 after you replaced the MAP sensor? Maybe it's just the old reading hanging around to haunt you.||||reg||5
